1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,261
Hi, this is Longview.

2
00:00:01,261 --> 00:00:06,053
It’s a mobile Web app for those times when you’re trying to make progress on several big goals at once.

3
00:00:06,053 --> 00:00:11,145
What you’re seeing today is our interactive prototype, so let me show you the experience we’re building.

4
00:00:11,145 --> 00:00:16,782
The problem with most planning tools is that they give every goal its own list, as if your time were unlimited.

5
00:00:16,782 --> 00:00:19,305
Longview starts with the time you actually have.

6
00:00:19,305 --> 00:00:27,303
In this example, I have twelve hours this week, split between building a SaaS startup, learning applied AI, and preparing to build a house.

7
00:00:27,303 --> 00:00:32,187
I can immediately see what I’m prioritizing, and what I’m choosing not to prioritize yet.

8
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:33,657
Now there’s a conflict.

9
00:00:33,657 --> 00:00:38,541
My startup interviews and my AI evaluation both want the same morning.

10
00:00:38,541 --> 00:00:43,391
Clara looks across my goals, my available time, and the decisions I’ve already made.

11
00:00:43,391 --> 00:00:49,224
She suggests keeping the customer interview and moving the more flexible AI task to Wednesday.

12
00:00:49,224 --> 00:00:52,152
But Clara doesn’t just go ahead and change my calendar.

13
00:00:52,152 --> 00:00:58,333
First, she shows me exactly what will move, where it will go, and why she thinks that’s the better tradeoff.

14
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:01,388
I can apply the change, adjust it, or cancel it.

15
00:01:01,388 --> 00:01:02,719
I stay in control.

16
00:01:02,719 --> 00:01:05,405
Once I approve it, Today updates with the new plan.

17
00:01:05,405 --> 00:01:10,740
Longview also keeps the reason behind that change, so I don’t have to reconstruct my thinking a week later.

18
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:14,525
And if I tap twice, the same task won’t get moved twice.

19
00:01:14,525 --> 00:01:17,407
Longview keeps research connected to the goal too.

20
00:01:17,407 --> 00:01:23,009
Clara can bring me useful evidence, but I decide what to accept, reject, or save for later.

21
00:01:23,009 --> 00:01:26,736
Anything I accept becomes a proposed update to the Goal Brief.

22
00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:29,641
I can still edit it before it becomes part of the plan.

23
00:01:29,641 --> 00:01:33,483
And when I finally reach a goal, Longview helps me capture what worked.

24
00:01:33,483 --> 00:01:37,256
I choose what Clara is allowed to remember and reuse next time.

25
00:01:37,256 --> 00:01:40,416
Nothing private is carried forward without my permission.

26
00:01:40,416 --> 00:01:42,800
We’ve also designed for the messy moments.

27
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:46,585
If I’m offline, my work stays clearly marked as waiting to sync.

28
00:01:46,585 --> 00:01:52,824
If Clara times out or returns something invalid, my last approved plan stays exactly as it was.

29
00:01:52,824 --> 00:01:58,657
That’s Longview: a way to pursue several ambitions without pretending they all fit at once.

30
00:01:58,657 --> 00:02:04,583
Clara helps me think across the whole picture, but I make every decision that actually changes my plan.

